d him it  might be lyme disease because of the  timing  and he actually drew blood uh and and  so my monocytes are up and said yep  you've got a bacterial in this case  spirochete infection  uh he recommended i take penicillin i  said no i need doxycycline  and it was tense for a few moments there  but he did give in give me give me the  doxycycline i cleared it right up and  then  when i got back uh it was roughly 10  days later  i basically had my blood drawn sure  enough i was lying positive  and it's a very well controlled  experiment i happened to have given  blood several days before i left i was  lying  negative so i'd see it converted during  that time so the point out of all this  is that i was able to pick up my lyme  disease  from a simple smart watch and pulse ox  which measure your blood oxygen  and so that prompted us to look at all  the different days i've been wearing my  smart watches about two years of date at  a time now it's about seven years a day  maybe eight years i guess it's eight  years we have a ton of data  and the point out of all this is that uh  when  if you look at the left side here it  turns out during the two years i've been  wearing the smart watch  there were five sorry there were four  times when i got ill there's the line  time those are the triangle days if you  will  and there were two times when i had a  definitely a viral infection that star  in the square at the bottom we now know  it's a viral infection  and the one with the circle was the time  when i was actually ill i had high  c-reactive protein or crp  but they didn't report being ill so  wasn't necessarily  strongly symptomatic to the point where  he called being healed but turns out my  crp was just as high  as any of those other times and the  point out of this plot is that if you  look at what's there  every single time i was ill with high  crp  i had a higher percentage of outlying  skin temperature  and heart rate every single time and so  the point out of this