e world again  and because i travel a lot we have  samples from all around the world  and as they say what i'm going to do is  tell you about both the biologicals and  chemical exposures people have  been exposed to so as i mentioned we  sequenced quite deeply  to figure out the biologicals and in  total we went up  doing over 70 billion reads it's really  quite a deep data set  about 30 million on the rna per sample  and 40 million on the  on the dna side  and what we've discovered is that over  2500 different species  are detected amongst the whole group but  it's quite a few even for one person so  the one person wearing the exposometer  for three months  had over a thousand species i've had  over 2000 from the first two years of  data so  you really expose a vast wealth of  microbes and other things out there  and we've looked at this in detail  that's shown here  so on p1 there's p2 there's p3 and this  is just at the kingdom level  each color code's a different kingdom so  red is bacterial  that brownish color is fungi green as  plants and  blue is animals and so what we  discovered is that the exposure  is very dynamic so it changes a lot from  one sample to the next  down below is clustering by sample types  of the plant samples  plant-dominated samples of the left  bacteria right next to that and brown  are the fungal ones  and so basically uh you can see what  kinds of exposures you're getting some  even for all for all three people they  expose expose them it's very very  dynamic  and it's fairly reproducible too though  so three times there's a monorail those  little arrows at the bottom  those are virtually always fungal  exposure so fungal exposures are quite  common  meaning they're they're the dominant  organism that you breathe  so then we went in one of the quantify  exactly what  is it that you're exposed to how much  comes from location how much from season  and so on and so forth  if you look at the on the left of my  samples you can see there's