ant in those samples  pesticides are everywhere but they're  much more in some locations than others  carcinogens are also everywhere like  diathlon glycol and a few others so  again but they're in some locations a  much much stronger rigid location  is the number one driver of your  chemical exposure  there are some interesting patterns we  see this next slide just shows  um a chemical that correlates with  rain so you may know you can smell when  it's going to rain  that's because of that top compound  josephine which  uh it actually comes out of the ground  that's a volatile for the air pressure  drops when it's going to rain  and so what will happen is this compound  will come out of the ground you can  smell it  that's how you know it's going to rain  and everywhere where that bar is up that  was a rainy day  and it turns out pesticides also come up  they must be volatile coming out of the  ground as well would be my guess when  the air pressure drops when it rains  and other things like plastics go down  they probably  got washed into the ground with my  guests and so  those exposures are less when it rains  we also could correlate biologicals and  chemicals and this is one of the most  interesting results  it turns out we discovered there's an  anti-correlation  between fungal exposures and this nasty  chemical  called pyridine which is usually found  in paints than other locations  and so if you look at this slide here  the bottom row shows pyridine  when it's when and where it's most  prevalent and the upper row shows  shows the fungal species and you'll  notice  everywhere where pyridine is low fungal  exposures are higher  and everywhere where pyridine is higher  fungal exposures are lower so  we assume then that pyridine is probably  um  killing fungi and so this is actually  kind of useful to know because  maybe if you're allergic to black mold  you'd actually want to  have your house painted with a pyridine  based paint and you could actually keep  off